<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vassal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as vassalage</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A vassal is a ruler or lord who holds power or land in subordination to a superior, owing loyalty, tribute or military service in return for protection or recognition. Vassalage was a widespread means of extending imperial authority without direct administration. It underpinned the structure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> ruled much of the Indonesian archipelago through vassal relationships.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vassal.jpg" alt="Vassal" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene23_Harold_sacramentum_fecit_Willelmo_duci.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A vassal, or liege subject, stands in a relation of mutual obligation to a superior lord or monarch, the suzerain. The rights and duties of the vassal are called vassalage, and those of the suzerain suzerainty. A vassal is semi-independent and may rule his own country as he wishes as long as he meets his obligations. These often include paying tribute or providing military support, with knights. In return the vassal received privileges such as commerce, military protection and land held as a tenant, called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. Fealty, by contrast, is the sworn, unconditional loyalty owed to a monarch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In developed European vassalage, lord and vassal took part in a commendation ceremony of two parts, homage and fealty, using Christian sacraments to show its sacred importance. According to Eginhard, the commendation made to Pippin the Younger in 757 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassilo-iii-of-bavaria">Tassilo III, Duke of Bavaria</a>, involved the relics of several saints assembled at Compiegne. Such refinements were not present from the outset, in times of crisis, war and hunger, when the weakest needed the protection of the knights who owned the weapons and knew how to fight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, who ruled from 768 to 814, vassalage slowly became connected with grants of land, then the main form of wealth. In Merovingian times monarchs rewarded only their most trusted vassals with lands, and even in 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> most vassals still had no fixed estates. Vassals later divided into an upper group of great territorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, strong enough to pass their benefice to their heirs, and a lower group of landless knights attached to a count or duke. As coordinated cavalry replaced disorganised infantry, armies became costlier, and a vassal needed land and its peasants, wood and water to equip the cavalry he owed his lord.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vassal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At that point the Persian kingdom was a minor <b>vassal</b> of the Median Empire under Astyages; within thirty years it would become the largest empire the world had yet seen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The emir ruled as a hereditary <b>vassal</b> of the Abbasid caliph, whose name was spoken in the Friday prayers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its influence stretched over the Nuba Mountains, the Butana, the Gash Delta, where a <b>vassal</b> king ruled, and, by the accounts of Ibn Hawqal and al-Aswani, the Eastern Desert to the Red Sea, lands once under Aksum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hōjō monopoly of power, and the lack of a reward of lands after the defeat of the Mongol invasions, left their <b>vassals</b> resentful.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 740s BCE the reforms of Tiglath-pileser III broke up large provinces and turned many <b>vassal</b> states into directly administered territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Bayinnaung forced Ayutthaya's submission in 1564 and took the city in 1569 after a long siege, making it a <b>vassal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
